Wang Zhouyun, Chen Jinming, Liu Changhua, and Zhang Wenqi — illegal detention case

Appellant: Wang Zhouyun, male, 48 years old, retired worker, from Jiangjin City, Sichuan Province. Arrested on August 29, 1996.

Appellant: Chen Jinming, male, 44 years old, from Neijiang City, Sichuan Province. Manager of Chongqing Jinchang Consignment Store. Arrested on August 26, 1996.

Defendant at First Instance: Liu Changhua, male, 32 years old, unemployed, from Emeishan City, Sichuan Province. Previously sentenced to five years of fixed-term imprisonment for robbery in 1989, released upon completion of sentence on December 20, 1993. Arrested on August 29, 1996.

Defendant at First Instance: Zhang Wenqi, male, 34 years old, from Guang'an County, Sichuan Province. Employee of Chongqing Wanjia Home Appliances Store. Arrested on August 26, 1996.

The Chongqing Intermediate People's Court of Sichuan Province convicted the defendant Wang Zhouyun of the crime of kidnapping for ransom, sentencing him to life imprisonment, deprivation of political rights for life, and a fine of RMB 10,000; and ordering the confiscation of one Motorola 8900 mobile phone used as a tool of the crime. It convicted the defendant Chen Jinming of the crime of kidnapping for ransom, sentencing him to life imprisonment, deprivation of political rights for life, and a fine of RMB 10,000; and ordering restitution to the victim Wei Wenchun in the amount of RMB 20,000. It convicted the defendant Zhang Wenqi of the crime of kidnapping for ransom, sentencing him to thirteen years of fixed-term imprisonment, deprivation of political rights for three years, and a fine of RMB 2,000; and ordering restitution to the victim Wei Wenchun in the amount of RMB 4,000. It convicted the defendant Liu Changhua of the crime of kidnapping for ransom, sentencing him to thirteen years of fixed-term imprisonment, deprivation of political rights for three years, and a fine of RMB 2,000; and ordering restitution to the victim Wei Wenchun in the amount of RMB 2,000. After the judgment was pronounced, defendants Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming did not accept it. Wang Zhouyun appealed to the Sichuan High People's Court on the grounds that his subjective motive was to recover due compensation and he did not have the purpose of extorting property, and that the first instance judgment was erroneous in nature and excessive in penalty, requesting a lighter punishment. Chen Jinming appealed on the grounds that he had only called Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi at Wang Zhouyun's request to help Wang collect a debt, and that he did not participate in the specific actions, and therefore did not commit the crime of kidnapping for ransom.

The Sichuan High People's Court found through trial: In early 1996, appellant Wang Zhouyun and the victim Wei Wenchun discussed jointly leasing a stall on the second floor of Jinying Market in Yuzhong District. Later, Wang Zhouyun was hospitalized for illness. Wei Wenchun then independently signed a lease contract with Jinying Market and paid a deposit and the initial rental fee. At the end of March, Wang Zhouyun recovered and was discharged from the hospital. He was dissatisfied with Wei's unilateral leasing of the market and insisted on investing. Out of courtesy, Wei on April 3 formally signed a joint venture agreement with Wang Zhouyun, stipulating that Wang Zhouyun should invest RMB 1.2 million. Subsequently, because Wang Zhouyun had no funds to invest, he privately transferred his investment share under the agreement to appellant Chen Jinming and three other persons, Wang (another person) and Wu (another person), for investment. After learning of this, Wei Wenchun strongly opposed. Through mediation by others, it was negotiated that Wei Wenchun would compensate Chen, Wang (the other person), and Wu each RMB 100,000, and also give Wang Zhouyun an additional compensation of RMB 50,000, after which Wang Zhouyun and the other three would withdraw from the joint venture. Later, Wei orally agreed over the phone to compensate Wang Zhouyun RMB 30,000, but when he learned that Wang would receive "benefits" from Chen Jinming, Wang (the other person), Wu (the other person), and others, he no longer agreed to compensate him. In early May, Wang Zhouyun told Chen Jinming that Wei Wenchun had not given him compensation and asked Chen to find someone to demand RMB 50,000 in compensation from Wei. Chen agreed. On May 12, Chen Jinming asked defendant at first instance Liu Changhua to find two persons to help Wang Zhouyun collect the debt, and also asked defendant at first instance Zhang Wenqi to go along, with specific actions to be arranged by Wang Zhouyun. On the morning of the 14th, Liu Changhua carried an imitation Japanese military saber, and together with Zhou Yu and Zheng Yu (both dealt with in separate cases), went to Chen Jinming's office to meet Zhang Wenqi and Wang Zhouyun. Wang Zhouyun told Liu, Zhang, and the others that Wei Wenchun owed him RMB 50,000, and asked them to help him collect the debt. Wang told Liu, Zhang, and the others to wait in Chen Jinming's office while he went out to look for Wei. At approximately 2:00 p.m. that day, Wang Zhouyun found Wei Wenchun in a leisure room of Chongqing Sanyuan Department Store, and paged to notify Liu, Zhang, and the others. Liu Changhua, Zhang Wenqi, Zheng Yu, and Zhou Yu arrived carrying a knife and a homemade handgun. Zhang Wenqi tricked Wei into coming out on the pretext of having something to discuss. The four then told Wei to repay Wang Zhouyun RMB 50,000. Wei denied it. Liu Changhua and Zhou Yu threatened Wei with the knife and gun, forcing him to go with them. During the pushing and pulling, Wei's right finger was cut and bled by Liu's knife. The four forced Wei out onto the street, took two taxis, and transported Wei to the female friend's home of Liu at Dongfeng Machinery Factory Family Quarters, Shangqiao, Zhangjiawan, Jiulongpo District. Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi forced Wei to come up with RMB 50,000 in cash within the day before he would be released. Wei, under duress, used a mobile phone provided by Zhang to contact his friend Qi and others to borrow money, but only borrowed RMB 30,000. Liu and Zhang telephoned Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming, and Wang and Chen agreed to take the RMB 30,000 first. Liu and Zhang then demanded that Wei write an IOU for another RMB 30,000, and also demanded that Wei take off his diamond ring, gold necklace, and jade pendant that he was wearing as collateral. At approximately 7:00 p.m. that day, Wei Wenchun's driver, Guan, brought the borrowed RMB 30,000 in cash and, together with Wang Zhouyun and others, went to Shangqiao Bus Station in Jiulongpo District to meet Zhang Wenqi and Liu Changhua to negotiate the release. Because Guan did not see Wei, he was unwilling to give the money first. After Wang Zhouyun mediated and provided a guarantee, Guan gave the RMB 30,000 cash to Wang. Only then did Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi notify Zheng and Zhou to release Wei Wenchun. At approximately 9:00 p.m. that day, Wang Zhouyun contacted Chen Jinming and then went to the Jinlong Hotel in Yuzhong District to hand over the RMB 30,000 to Chen Jinming. Chen distributed RMB 10,000 to Liu, Zhang, and the others as "payment for their trouble." The next day, Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming returned the gold jewelry and IOU to Wei Wenchun and apologized to him. A few days later, fearing further trouble, Wei Wenchun reported the incident.

The above facts are confirmed by the victim Wei Wenchun's report and statement, the testimony of witnesses including Wang (the other person), Wu (the other person), Qi, and Guan, and by seized physical evidence and documentary evidence. The appellants and the defendants at first instance also have recorded confessions in the case file. The facts are clear and the evidence is sufficient.

The Sichuan High People's Court held: Appellants Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming, in order to demand compensation that the victim Wei Wenchun had orally promised, invited defendants at first instance Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi and others to forcibly take Wei elsewhere, restrict his personal freedom, and demand property. Their conduct violated Article 143, paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law and constituted the crime of illegal detention. In the joint crime, Wang Zhouyun played a principal role and was a principal offender, and should be given a heavier punishment in accordance with Article 22, paragraph 1 and Article 23 of the Criminal Law. Chen Jinming, Liu Changhua, and Zhang Wenqi played secondary roles and were accomplices, and should be given a lighter punishment in accordance with Article 22, paragraph 1 and Article 24 of the Criminal Law. Liu Changhua was a recidivist, and in accordance with Article 61 of the Criminal Law, should be given a heavier punishment. After hearing Wang Zhouyun say that Wei Wenchun owed him RMB 50,000, Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi on their own initiative forced Wei to write an IOU and used Wei's jewelry as collateral. For this, Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming should not bear responsibility. Wang Zhouyun and Chen Jinming returned the IOU and jewelry to Wei Wenchun the next day. The conduct of Liu Changhua and Zhang Wenqi in forcing Wei to write an IOU and using his jewelry as collateral did not cause serious consequences, and the two were accomplices in this case, so no separate conviction was made. The first instance did not ascertain the fact that Wei Wenchun had made a promise and then reneged, and that Wang Zhouyun committed the crime for the purpose of recovering the compensation promised by Wei Wenchun. Based on this, it found that the conduct of Wang Zhouyun, Chen Jinming, Zhang Wenqi, and Liu Changhua constituted the crime of kidnapping for ransom under Article 2, paragraphs 1 and 3 of the Decision of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress on Severely Punishing Criminals Who Abduct and Traffic in or Kidnap Women or Children. This constituted a failure to find some facts clearly, leading to errors in characterization and application of law, and should be corrected. Accordingly, in accordance with Article 189, item (3) of the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Sichuan High People's Court on May 28, 1997, rendered the following judgment:

1. The criminal judgment of first instance is reversed;

2. Appellant Wang Zhouyun is convicted of illegal detention and sentenced to three years of fixed-term imprisonment;

3. Appellant Chen Jinming is convicted of illegal detention and sentenced to two years of fixed-term imprisonment;

4. Defendant at first instance Liu Changhua is convicted of illegal detention and sentenced to two years and six months of fixed-term imprisonment;

5. Defendant at first instance Zhang Wenqi is convicted of illegal detention and sentenced to two years of fixed-term imprisonment;

6. The seized tools of the crime, one imitation Japanese military saber and one self-made revolver, are to be confiscated.
